Initialize configuration|terraform init|Initializes the backend, providers and modules for a root configuration.|Prepare a checked-out configuration before validation or planning.|Read backend prompts and provider selections; initialization success is not infrastructure creation.|Terraform has been successfully initialized!|Can download code and configure or migrate state; use a trusted configuration and review prompts.
Format check|terraform fmt -check -recursive|Checks canonical formatting in the directory tree without rewriting files.|Keep formatting consistent in review and CI.|Listed filenames need formatting; a nonzero exit signals a formatting issue.|main.tf|Does not apply infrastructure changes; run from the intended repository root.
Validate configuration|terraform validate|Checks configuration syntax and internal consistency after initialization.|Catch invalid arguments and references before planning.|Success confirms structural validity, not credentials, quotas or remote runtime correctness.|Success! The configuration is valid.|Read-only validation; provider dependencies must be available.
Review planned changes|terraform plan -out=tfplan|Creates a plan and saves it for a later apply.|Review the actual change set before infrastructure mutation.|+ creates, ~ updates, - deletes and -/+ replaces; inspect each replacement and summary.|Plan: 1 to add, 1 to change, 0 to destroy.|Refresh reads remote APIs; saved plans can contain secrets and require protected storage.
Inspect saved plan|terraform show tfplan|Displays a saved plan in human-readable form.|Review a plan artifact without applying it.|Check addresses, before/after values and unknown values marked known after apply.|# resource address will be updated in-place|Read-only; plan content can contain sensitive information.
Apply reviewed plan|terraform apply tfplan|Executes the actions in a saved plan.|Deploy exactly the reviewed plan instead of generating a new interactive one.|Inspect per-resource progress and final result; errors may leave partial successful changes.|Apply complete! Resources: ...|Mutates infrastructure and does not ask for fresh interactive approval for a saved plan; confirm account, workspace and plan provenance first.
List managed addresses|terraform state list|Lists resource addresses recorded in the selected state.|Locate the exact address before inspection or migration.|Addresses include module paths and instance keys; presence does not guarantee remote health.|module.web.aws_instance.app["blue"]|Read-only state access; verify backend and workspace.
Inspect managed object|terraform state show 'RESOURCE_ADDRESS'|Displays recorded attributes for one managed resource.|Compare recorded identity with the remote object.|The id identifies the remote object; values are recorded state and may need refresh.|id = "resource-id"|Read-only but may reveal sensitive data; replace and correctly quote the address.
Show outputs|terraform output|Displays root-module output values.|Find published endpoints or identifiers after deployment.|Sensitive values are normally redacted in this human-readable view; raw or JSON modes can reveal them.|service_url = "https://..."|Read-only state access; avoid exposing secrets through alternate output modes.
Current workspace|terraform workspace show|Prints the selected CLI workspace.|Confirm environment selection before planning or applying.|The name identifies a state selection, not an independent permission boundary.|production|Read-only; also verify credentials and backend configuration.
Provider inventory|terraform providers|Shows provider requirements in configuration and state.|Investigate unexpected plugin dependencies or upgrade scope.|Compare provider source addresses and version constraints across modules.|provider[registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws]|Read-only; actual selected versions are recorded in the lock file.
Drift-only preview|terraform plan -refresh-only|Plans state and output reconciliation with remote changes without proposing normal configuration reconciliation.|Review out-of-band changes before deciding to adopt or revert them.|Changes describe the refreshed view; applying this plan updates state rather than restoring configuration intent.|Objects have changed outside of Terraform|Reads remote APIs; do not treat accepting drift as a security or correctness review.
CLI version and platform|terraform version|Reports Terraform and selected provider versions.|Establish the exact toolchain behind a differing plan or CI failure.|The Terraform version and platform describe the executable; provider versions may appear for the initialized directory.|Terraform v1.x.x on linux_amd64|Read-only; compare the version with project constraints and the CI runner.
Machine-readable validation|terraform validate -json|Checks configuration and emits structured diagnostics.|Integrate syntax and internal-consistency checks into CI.|valid is a boolean; diagnostics include severity, summary and source range. This does not validate remote runtime behavior.|{"valid":true,"error_count":0}|Requires initialization for referenced providers/modules; no infrastructure changes are applied.
Detect planned changes|terraform plan -detailed-exitcode|Creates a plan with distinct exit codes for changes and errors.|Let CI distinguish a clean plan from actionable drift.|Exit 0 means no changes, 1 means error, and 2 means changes. Do not treat 2 as an execution failure automatically.|Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.|Reads remote state and may acquire a lock; confirm workspace, backend and credentials.
Plan with environment inputs|terraform plan -var-file=staging.tfvars|Plans using an explicit variable file.|Review the effect of a chosen environment's inputs.|The resource diff reflects merged inputs and configuration; the filename alone does not enforce environment isolation.|Plan: 0 to add, 1 to change, 0 to destroy.|May reveal sensitive values if not marked; verify backend and credentials separately from the variable file.
Controlled provider upgrade|terraform init -upgrade|Reinitializes dependencies while considering newer versions within configured constraints.|Evaluate an intentional provider/module dependency update.|Inspect changed lock selections and a subsequent plan; initialization success does not establish resource compatibility.|Terraform has made some changes to the provider dependency selections...|Changes local dependency selections and lock file; perform in a reviewable branch and honor project version policy.
Backend reconfiguration|terraform init -reconfigure|Reinitializes backend configuration without migrating prior state automatically.|Connect a working directory to an intentionally chosen existing backend.|Backend configured successfully refers to the new configuration, not proof that the expected resource state is present.|Successfully configured the backend...|Can point subsequent operations at different state. Verify destination and expected state before planning; do not confuse with state migration.
Lock selections for platforms|terraform providers lock -platform=linux_amd64 -platform=darwin_arm64|Records provider checksums for selected execution platforms.|Prepare a shared lock file for reviewed developer and CI environments.|The lock file gains hashes for the requested platforms; unsupported provider platforms cause errors.|Obtained provider checksums for linux_amd64|Writes the lock file and contacts registries; inspect changes and keep provider constraints intentional.
Provider dependency tree|terraform providers schema -json|Exports the initialized providers' configuration and resource schemas.|Inspect supported attributes for tooling or version comparisons.|provider_schemas maps provider addresses to blocks; required, optional and computed describe schema roles.|{"format_version":"1.0","provider_schemas":{...}}|Read-only local/schema inspection after initialization; schema is not a complete behavioral contract.
Dependency graph|terraform graph|Prints the configuration dependency graph in DOT format.|Inspect ordering or unexpected coupling between resources.|Directed edges express dependency relationships; feed DOT to a graph viewer if needed.|digraph { ... }|Read-only; labels can reveal infrastructure names. Graph output is not an execution success report.
Expression console|terraform console|Opens an interactive evaluator for Terraform expressions.|Test transformations and inspect expression results before editing configuration.|Enter an expression and read its typed result; exit leaves the console. Expressions can expose values loaded from state.|> length(["a", "b"]) / 2|Interactive and may access state; avoid displaying secrets or evaluating against the wrong workspace.
Structured outputs|terraform output -json|Emits root-module outputs as JSON metadata and values.|Feed verified outputs into downstream automation.|Each output includes type, value and sensitive status. JSON output can include sensitive values in plaintext.|{"endpoint":{"sensitive":false,"type":"string","value":"..."}}|Read-only but potentially secret-bearing; restrict logs and files containing this output.
Structured saved plan|terraform show -json tfplan|Converts a saved plan into machine-readable JSON.|Inspect actions or run policy analysis on the exact reviewed plan.|resource_changes contains planned actions; before/after values may include secrets even when the CLI redacts them.|"actions": ["update"]|Read-only conversion; protect plan and output artifacts with appropriate access and retention.
Workspace inventory|terraform workspace list|Lists CLI workspaces known to the current backend.|Check available state partitions before selecting one.|The asterisk marks the active workspace; names do not enforce separate credentials or security boundaries.|* default / staging|Read-only; backend configuration determines the workspace list.
Select an existing workspace|terraform workspace select staging|Switches the working directory to an existing workspace.|Choose the intended state partition before planning.|The selected message confirms the name, not its safety or credentials. Run workspace show and a reviewable plan afterward.|Switched to workspace "staging".|Changes local workspace selection and affects later operations; verify the backend and expected resources.
State backup export|terraform state pull|Writes the current state snapshot to standard output.|Take a controlled diagnostic backup before state operations.|JSON includes lineage and serial plus resource data; it is not a user-friendly inventory and may contain secrets.|{"version":4,"serial":42,"lineage":"..."}|Reads backend state. Save only to a protected destination and follow the backend's backup policy.
Import existing infrastructure|terraform import 'aws_instance.web' INSTANCE_ID|Associates an existing object with a resource address in state.|Bring a verified existing resource under management without creating a duplicate.|Import success means the association was recorded; review configuration and the next plan for unintended changes.|Import successful!|Mutates state, not a complete configuration authoring workflow. Back up state, confirm identity and import support first.
Unlock abandoned operation|terraform force-unlock LOCK_ID|Requests removal of a lock associated with a failed operation.|Recover access only after confirming no legitimate writer remains.|The prompt names the lock ID; successful unlock does not roll back a partial apply.|Terraform state has been successfully unlocked!|High-impact coordination change. Never use to bypass an active operation; verify backend, lock owner and job termination.
Module tests|terraform test|Runs test files defined for the configuration or module.|Exercise assertions about plans or provisioned resources in a controlled environment.|Run results identify passing/failing assertions; inspect cleanup messages for resources left behind.|Success! 2 passed, 0 failed.|Tests can create real billable infrastructure by default; use an isolated authorized account and inspect test run commands first.
